The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
String value = helper.getParameter(option.name());
Long id = option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), value);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because there is no null check for the `value` obtained from `helper.getParameter(option.name())`. If `value` is null, the subsequent call to `option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), value)` will lead to a failure when processing the query, particularly for single-choice parameters like SUBJECT or DEPARTMENT.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (value != null) {
    Long id = option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), value);
}
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a null check for `value` before attempting to look it up. This prevents the code from trying to process a null value, thereby avoiding the failures that occur when exporting HQL reports as CSV with single-choice parameters.

